::GEO_U02_L05_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation is rigid? {
= A translation#Correct. A translation matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ A dilation with scale factor 2#This choice reflects a misconception: A dilation with scale factor 2. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ A vertical stretch#This choice reflects a misconception: A vertical stretch. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ A horizontal compression#This choice reflects a misconception: A horizontal compression. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A rotation maps figure A exactly onto figure B. What conclusion is supported? {
~ Figure A is larger than figure B#This choice reflects a misconception: Figure A is larger than figure B. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= Figure A &cong; figure B#Correct. Figure A &cong; figure B matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Figure A is only similar to figure B#This choice reflects a misconception: Figure A is only similar to figure B. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Figure A has changed angle measures#This choice reflects a misconception: Figure A has changed angle measures. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which evidence best justifies congruence? {
~ The figures look close in size#This choice reflects a misconception: The figures look close in size. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ The figures are drawn in the same color#This choice reflects a misconception: The figures are drawn in the same color. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= A reflection maps one figure exactly onto the other#Correct. A reflection maps one figure exactly onto the other matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ One figure appears above the other#This choice reflects a misconception: One figure appears above the other. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Why does a translation support congruence? {
~ It changes side lengths#This choice reflects a misconception: It changes side lengths. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ It changes angle measures#This choice reflects a misconception: It changes angle measures. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ It makes a figure similar only#This choice reflects a misconception: It makes a figure similar only. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= It preserves distance and angle measure#Correct. It preserves distance and angle measure matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A dilation with scale factor 3 maps one figure to another. What should you conclude? {
= This does not justify congruence#Correct. This does not justify congruence matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ The figures are congruent by rigid motion#This choice reflects a misconception: The figures are congruent by rigid motion. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ The figures have equal side lengths#This choice reflects a misconception: The figures have equal side lengths. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ The transformation is rigid#This choice reflects a misconception: The transformation is rigid. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q06::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A reflection followed by a rotation maps triangle ABC onto triangle DEF. What is true? {
~ Only the angles changed#This choice reflects a misconception: Only the angles changed. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= Triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF#Correct. Triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Triangle DEF is larger#This choice reflects a misconception: Triangle DEF is larger. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ The sequence is non-rigid#This choice reflects a misconception: The sequence is non-rigid. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q07::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which phrase belongs in a congruence justification? {
~ because it looks the same#This choice reflects a misconception: because it looks the same. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ because it has matching color#This choice reflects a misconception: because it has matching color. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= because a rigid transformation maps one figure onto the other#Correct. because a rigid transformation maps one figure onto the other matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ because the figure moved somewhere else#This choice reflects a misconception: because the figure moved somewhere else. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q08::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>If one figure can be translated onto another, what is preserved? {
~ Only orientation#This choice reflects a misconception: Only orientation. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Only location#This choice reflects a misconception: Only location. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Only color#This choice reflects a misconception: Only color. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= Distance and angle measure#Correct. Distance and angle measure matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q09::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which sequence can justify congruence? {
= Reflect, then translate#Correct. Reflect, then translate matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Dilate by 2, then translate#This choice reflects a misconception: Dilate by 2, then translate. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Stretch vertically, then rotate#This choice reflects a misconception: Stretch vertically, then rotate. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Compress horizontally, then reflect#This choice reflects a misconception: Compress horizontally, then reflect. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q10::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A student says two figures are congruent because they look alike. What is missing? {
= Transformation evidence#Correct. Transformation evidence matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ A color label#This choice reflects a misconception: A color label. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ A larger diagram#This choice reflects a misconception: A larger diagram. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ A similarity statement only#This choice reflects a misconception: A similarity statement only. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q11::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A rotation is used in a congruence proof because it is what type of transformation? {
~ Non-rigid#This choice reflects a misconception: Non-rigid. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= Rigid#Correct. Rigid matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Dilation#This choice reflects a misconception: Dilation. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Scale change#This choice reflects a misconception: Scale change. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q12::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which transformation does not preserve distance? {
~ Reflection#This choice reflects a misconception: Reflection. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Translation#This choice reflects a misconception: Translation. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= Dilation with scale factor 2#Correct. Dilation with scale factor 2 matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Rotation#This choice reflects a misconception: Rotation. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q13::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A rigid motion maps quadrilateral ABCD onto quadrilateral WXYZ. Which conclusion is valid? {
= ABCD &cong; WXYZ#Correct. ABCD &cong; WXYZ matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ ABCD is larger than WXYZ#This choice reflects a misconception: ABCD is larger than WXYZ. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Only one pair of sides is equal#This choice reflects a misconception: Only one pair of sides is equal. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ No corresponding parts match#This choice reflects a misconception: No corresponding parts match. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q14::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>What does it mean for one figure to map onto another? {
= It lands exactly on the other figure#Correct. It lands exactly on the other figure matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ It changes into any nearby figure#This choice reflects a misconception: It changes into any nearby figure. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ It becomes a different size#This choice reflects a misconception: It becomes a different size. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ It loses corresponding vertices#This choice reflects a misconception: It loses corresponding vertices. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q15::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which is not mathematical evidence for congruence? {
~ A translation rule#This choice reflects a misconception: A translation rule. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ A matching rigid-motion sequence#This choice reflects a misconception: A matching rigid-motion sequence. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= A statement that figures look congruent#Correct. A statement that figures look congruent matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ A rotation mapping one figure onto another#This choice reflects a misconception: A rotation mapping one figure onto another. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q16::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A figure is rotated 180 degrees and lands exactly on another figure. Why are the figures congruent? {
~ Rotation changes all distances#This choice reflects a misconception: Rotation changes all distances. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= Rotation is rigid and preserves the figure#Correct. Rotation is rigid and preserves the figure matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Rotation creates only similarity#This choice reflects a misconception: Rotation creates only similarity. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Rotation changes the number of sides#This choice reflects a misconception: Rotation changes the number of sides. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q17::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A sequence includes a reflection and a dilation with scale factor 1.5. Can this sequence justify congruence? {
~ Yes, because every transformation proves congruence#This choice reflects a misconception: Yes, because every transformation proves congruence. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Yes, because reflection is included#This choice reflects a misconception: Yes, because reflection is included. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= No, because the dilation changes distance#Correct. No, because the dilation changes distance matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ No, because reflection changes angle measure#This choice reflects a misconception: No, because reflection changes angle measure. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q18::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>If triangle ABC &cong; triangle DEF by rigid motion, what must be true? {
= Corresponding sides and angles match#Correct. Corresponding sides and angles match matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ The triangles have different sizes#This choice reflects a misconception: The triangles have different sizes. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Only one vertex matches#This choice reflects a misconception: Only one vertex matches. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ The triangles are unrelated#This choice reflects a misconception: The triangles are unrelated. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q19::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly uses rigid transformation language? {
~ A stretch maps one congruent figure to another#This choice reflects a misconception: A stretch maps one congruent figure to another. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
= A rotation maps triangle ABC exactly onto triangle DEF, so the triangles are congruent#Correct. A rotation maps triangle ABC exactly onto triangle DEF, so the triangles are congruent matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ The figures are congruent because they are both triangles#This choice reflects a misconception: The figures are congruent because they are both triangles. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ A dilation by 4 proves congruence#This choice reflects a misconception: A dilation by 4 proves congruence. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q20::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A translation maps A to A prime, B to B prime, and C to C prime. What does this preserve in triangle ABC? {
= Side lengths and angle measures#Correct. Side lengths and angle measures matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Only x-coordinates#This choice reflects a misconception: Only x-coordinates. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Only y-coordinates#This choice reflects a misconception: Only y-coordinates. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Only the names of the vertices#This choice reflects a misconception: Only the names of the vertices. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q21::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Which pair lists only rigid transformations? {
= Translation and reflection#Correct. Translation and reflection matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Dilation and stretch#This choice reflects a misconception: Dilation and stretch. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Compression and dilation#This choice reflects a misconception: Compression and dilation. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Stretch and reflection#This choice reflects a misconception: Stretch and reflection. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q22::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>Why is a reflection a valid step in a congruence justification? {
= It preserves distance even though orientation may change#Correct. It preserves distance even though orientation may change matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ It doubles side lengths#This choice reflects a misconception: It doubles side lengths. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ It changes every angle measure#This choice reflects a misconception: It changes every angle measure. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ It removes corresponding parts#This choice reflects a misconception: It removes corresponding parts. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q23::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>A student claims a dilation proves congruence because the shape stays the same. What is the correction? {
= Congruence also requires same size, so a dilation with scale factor not 1 is not enough#Correct. Congruence also requires same size, so a dilation with scale factor not 1 is not enough matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Shape is never relevant#This choice reflects a misconception: Shape is never relevant. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Dilation is always rigid#This choice reflects a misconception: Dilation is always rigid. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Only color matters for congruence#This choice reflects a misconception: Only color matters for congruence. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q24::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>What should a complete congruence justification include? {
= A named rigid transformation and a congruence conclusion#Correct. A named rigid transformation and a congruence conclusion matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ Only a drawing#This choice reflects a misconception: Only a drawing. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Only the word congruent#This choice reflects a misconception: Only the word congruent. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ Only one side length#This choice reflects a misconception: Only one side length. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}

::GEO_U02_L05_Q25::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L05_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.6<br><br>Question:<br>If no rigid transformation maps one figure exactly onto another, what cannot be justified from that evidence? {
= Congruence by rigid motion#Correct. Congruence by rigid motion matches the lesson evidence and supports the mapped standard for this question.
~ The number of vertices#This choice reflects a misconception: The number of vertices. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ The figure names#This choice reflects a misconception: The figure names. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
~ The coordinate plane location#This choice reflects a misconception: The coordinate plane location. Recheck the given evidence and apply the lesson standard before choosing a conclusion.
}
